1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,895
A box is twelve centimetres
long, four wide and three tall.

2
00:00:03,895 --> 00:00:06,705
Can a pencil thirteen
centimetres long fit inside?

3
00:00:07,105 --> 00:00:10,839
Lay it flat on the floor of
the box, from corner to corner.

4
00:00:10,839 --> 00:00:14,646
The longest flat line is only
about twelve point six centimetres.

5
00:00:14,646 --> 00:00:15,546
Too short.

6
00:00:15,892 --> 00:00:19,622
Aarav hit this puzzle at his study
desk, packing his pencil box.

7
00:00:19,972 --> 00:00:24,459
Then he tilted the pencil, from a
bottom corner up to the opposite top

8
00:00:24,459 --> 00:00:25,359
corner.

9
00:00:25,118 --> 00:00:26,447
It fit, exactly.

10
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:29,538
How do we measure a line
that cuts through space?

11
00:00:29,538 --> 00:00:32,027
Today: the distance formula
in three dimensions.

12
00:00:32,677 --> 00:00:34,128
Quick reminder first.

13
00:00:34,478 --> 00:00:37,407
In a room, every point
needs three numbers.

14
00:00:37,407 --> 00:00:40,700
How far along, how far
across, and how far up.

15
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:43,829
These are its coordinates: x, y and z.

16
00:00:43,829 --> 00:00:46,857
The corner where all
three start is the origin.

17
00:00:47,257 --> 00:00:48,667
Now watch the box.

18
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:53,008
We lift its lid slowly, so its
height grows from zero to three.

19
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:57,016
The corner to corner line
tilts up, and it gets longer.

20
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:59,539
At height zero it is twelve point six.

21
00:00:59,539 --> 00:01:02,053
At height three it is exactly thirteen.

22
00:01:02,703 --> 00:01:03,624
Why thirteen?

23
00:01:03,624 --> 00:01:07,027
We find it in two steps,
with a rule you already know.

24
00:01:07,377 --> 00:01:08,277
Pythagoras.

25
00:01:08,272 --> 00:01:13,040
In a right angled triangle, the longest
side squared equals the other two

26
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,684
sides squared, added.

27
00:01:15,084 --> 00:01:15,984
Step one.

28
00:01:15,893 --> 00:01:17,461
Walk across the floor.

29
00:01:17,461 --> 00:01:21,405
The floor diagonal squared is
twelve squared plus four squared.

30
00:01:21,405 --> 00:01:23,486
That is one hundred and sixty.

31
00:01:24,236 --> 00:01:25,136
Step two.

32
00:01:25,013 --> 00:01:25,913
Climb up.

33
00:01:25,789 --> 00:01:30,362
The floor diagonal and the height make
a new right triangle, standing straight

34
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:31,262
up.

35
00:01:31,548 --> 00:01:36,247
So the long diagonal squared is one
hundred and sixty, plus three squared.

36
00:01:36,247 --> 00:01:38,602
That is one hundred and sixty nine.

37
00:01:39,352 --> 00:01:43,093
The square root of one hundred
and sixty nine is thirteen.

38
00:01:43,093 --> 00:01:44,900
The pencil fits, exactly.

39
00:01:45,550 --> 00:01:47,535
Now take any two points.

40
00:01:47,535 --> 00:01:50,008
P is x one, y one, z one.

41
00:01:50,008 --> 00:01:52,481
Q is x two, y two, z two.

42
00:01:52,881 --> 00:01:56,225
Build a box with P and
Q at opposite corners.

43
00:01:56,225 --> 00:01:58,835
Its edges run along the three axes.

44
00:01:59,235 --> 00:02:02,186
The length of the box is the change in x.

45
00:02:02,186 --> 00:02:04,191
That is x two minus x one.

46
00:02:04,941 --> 00:02:09,205
The width is the change in y, and
the height is the change in z.

47
00:02:09,955 --> 00:02:13,006
Use Pythagoras twice,
just like the pencil.

48
00:02:13,006 --> 00:02:16,831
PQ squared is the sum of
the three changes, squared.

49
00:02:17,581 --> 00:02:18,823
Take the square root.

50
00:02:18,823 --> 00:02:21,390
That is the distance
formula in three dimensions.

51
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:25,689
If P is the origin, every
first number is zero.

52
00:02:25,689 --> 00:02:30,648
So the distance of a point from the
origin is the root of x squared plus

53
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:32,584
y squared plus z squared.

54
00:02:33,234 --> 00:02:34,221
Side by side.

55
00:02:34,221 --> 00:02:36,815
On a flat map, a point has two numbers.

56
00:02:36,815 --> 00:02:39,683
So the distance has two
squares under the root.

57
00:02:40,083 --> 00:02:42,520
In a room, a point has three numbers.

58
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:44,620
So the distance has three squares.

59
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:46,483
One square for each direction.

60
00:02:47,083 --> 00:02:48,013
Now you try.

61
00:02:48,013 --> 00:02:52,012
What is the distance from the
origin to the point one, one, one?

62
00:02:52,012 --> 00:02:53,682
Root two, or root three?

63
00:02:53,682 --> 00:02:54,838
Pause and decide.

64
00:02:58,038 --> 00:02:58,938
Root three!

65
00:02:58,861 --> 00:03:01,072
There are three changes of one each.

66
00:03:01,072 --> 00:03:03,058
One plus one plus one is three.

67
00:03:03,708 --> 00:03:04,803
One more question.

68
00:03:04,803 --> 00:03:06,966
Does it matter which point you call P?

69
00:03:07,366 --> 00:03:11,003
Is the distance from P to
Q the same as from Q to P?

70
00:03:11,003 --> 00:03:12,215
Pause and think.

71
00:03:15,415 --> 00:03:16,428
Yes, always!

72
00:03:16,428 --> 00:03:19,931
Minus five squared and five
squared are both twenty five.

73
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:22,118
Squaring wipes out the minus sign.

74
00:03:22,768 --> 00:03:23,769
Remember this.

75
00:03:23,769 --> 00:03:26,736
Three changes, three
squares, one square root.

76
00:03:27,436 --> 00:03:29,126
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:34,461
Find the distance between P, one,
minus three, four, and Q, minus four,

78
00:03:34,461 --> 00:03:35,382
one, two.

79
00:03:35,882 --> 00:03:36,782
Step one.

80
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:39,530
The change in x is minus four minus one.

81
00:03:39,530 --> 00:03:40,985
That is minus five.

82
00:03:41,735 --> 00:03:42,635
Step two.

83
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:45,235
The change in y is one minus minus three.

84
00:03:45,235 --> 00:03:46,565
That is plus four.

85
00:03:47,315 --> 00:03:48,215
Step three.

86
00:03:48,185 --> 00:03:50,485
The change in z is two minus four.

87
00:03:50,485 --> 00:03:51,800
That is minus two.

88
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:53,450
Step four.

89
00:03:53,373 --> 00:03:54,489
Square and add.

90
00:03:54,489 --> 00:03:57,554
Twenty five plus sixteen
plus four is forty five.

91
00:03:58,304 --> 00:03:59,204
Step five.

92
00:03:59,115 --> 00:04:02,760
The distance is root forty
five, which is three root five.

93
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,578
About six point seven units.

94
00:04:05,228 --> 00:04:06,689
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:07,039 --> 00:04:12,033
Find the distance between two, minus
one, three, and minus two, one, three.

96
00:04:12,033 --> 00:04:16,373
Many students write the change in
y as one minus one, which is zero.

97
00:04:16,773 --> 00:04:18,955
But the first y is minus one.

98
00:04:18,955 --> 00:04:22,619
So the change is one minus
minus one, which is two.

99
00:04:22,619 --> 00:04:25,634
The right answer is root twenty, not four.

100
00:04:26,284 --> 00:04:27,672
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:27,672 --> 00:04:30,643
In JEE Main, as in January
twenty twenty five.

102
00:04:30,643 --> 00:04:35,019
A point had to be the same distance
from three given points, and you find

103
00:04:35,019 --> 00:04:35,919
it.

104
00:04:36,048 --> 00:04:37,300
Exam level now.

105
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:40,314
Find the sum of all possible values of k.

106
00:04:40,314 --> 00:04:41,619
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:47,019 --> 00:04:48,479
The answer is A.

108
00:04:48,479 --> 00:04:49,379
Four.

109
00:04:49,613 --> 00:04:53,621
The changes are k minus two,
then two, then minus four.

110
00:04:54,121 --> 00:04:59,564
So k minus two, squared, plus four,
plus sixteen, equals six squared, thirty

111
00:04:59,564 --> 00:05:00,464
six.

112
00:05:00,605 --> 00:05:04,069
That gives k minus two,
squared, equals sixteen.

113
00:05:04,069 --> 00:05:07,138
So k minus two is plus four or minus four.

114
00:05:07,138 --> 00:05:10,530
k is six or minus two,
and their sum is four.

115
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:12,670
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:12,670 --> 00:05:14,253
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:17,453 --> 00:05:19,639
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:20,039 --> 00:05:20,939
One.

119
00:05:20,505 --> 00:05:23,939
How far is the point two,
three, six from the origin?

120
00:05:24,839 --> 00:05:25,739
Two.

121
00:05:25,306 --> 00:05:28,922
Why is the distance from P
to Q the same as from Q to P?

122
00:05:29,822 --> 00:05:30,722
Three.

123
00:05:30,296 --> 00:05:33,651
How many squares sit under the
root in three dimensions, and why?

124
00:05:34,751 --> 00:05:36,882
Next time: walk halfway to a wall.

125
00:05:36,882 --> 00:05:38,529
Then half of what is left.

126
00:05:38,529 --> 00:05:39,590
Again and again.

127
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:41,281
Do you ever touch the wall?

128
00:05:41,281 --> 00:05:42,790
We find out with limits.
